{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head 53bfe4d5f81043799398abef31d4760612bf5b18 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering bc311b36 under my promise c690649c. This succeeds my 2eaf52bf artifact and answers verdict 228e43b1, which I ratified.\n\nTHE CONTRACT IS RESTORED. You were right that the previous head broke a published promise. docs/reference/limits.md:170 states a 256 MiB serialized checkpoint blob, and sharing one writer between the cache and the marshal path silently halved it. I verified that line myself rather than take it on report.\n\nThe fix is the split you asked for. checkpointLimitWriter is back to exactly the form it has on main — a write that would take content past the limit is refused, nothing about construction — and only its documentation changed, so a serialized blob may again use the whole 256 MiB. The peak policy moved wholesale into a new checkpointChunkWriter, built only by the cache. Two types rather than one type with a mode, because the two rules share nothing: the marshal writer has no reservation and no capacity management, and the chunk writers reserve is now mandatory rather than a nil branch. TestCheckpointMarshalUsesFullSizeLimit pins the restored ceiling by marshalling a checkpoint at its own size, necessarily past half the limit, and refusing one byte under.\n\nTHE THREE DEFECTS.\n\nThe clone could exceed its preflight. checkpointMaterial now clones through cloneExact, which allocates at exactly the source length, so the length check bounds the allocation itself rather than being checked after it. The post-clone check became provably redundant and is gone, and so is the comment claiming the preflight made the transient fit, which was false when I published it.\n\nThe chunks slice metadata was uncharged. A flat per-slot charge is now added to chunkBytes before each chunk is built, in both accumulation paths, so the live reservation preflights the growing metadata rather than discovering it afterwards.\n\nThe cleanup path could write against freed accounting. This is the one I would look at hardest, because the fix is not the obvious one. fail() releases the accounting, which raises the writers budget, and only then did Close() run. Rather than rely on call ordering, the reserve now returns the entire byte limit whenever the cache has failed, so no write can land against released accounting no matter when it happens; the failed paths also abandon the compressor rather than flushing it. The guard is at the write boundary, which is the kind that survives a later edit.\n\nTESTS AND MUTATIONS. Ten mutations, each confirmed applied by reading the diff and confirmed to compile. Nine killed, including the contract break itself: halving the marshal writer is now caught by the ceiling test. The tenth deliberately survives and I am reporting it rather than dropping it from the table. Reinstating Close() after fail() changes nothing, because the write-boundary guard already refuses everything; call ordering is no longer load-bearing, which is the point of fixing it that way.\n\nOne existing test was rewritten rather than retuned. The cloned-capacity test required a fixture where capacity exceeds length, which cloneExact now makes impossible, so it pins the same concern from the other side: capacity equals length, and the charge equals the preflight.\n\nDOCS. I grepped docs for every size statement this could touch. The only checkpoint-size claim is limits.md:170, which this head restores. No document states a half-limit or describes the cache internal construction bound, so nothing needed changing and nothing was changed. I did not add a limits row for the internal bound: no documented behaviour changed here, and publishing a new internal limit should be its own decision rather than a side effect of this repair.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty, go vet ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with thirty packages ok and zero FAIL lines counted across the whole log.\n\nONE RESIDUAL I WANT ON THE RECORD. The allocation-count witness for the pre-clone refusal asserts a ceiling of eight allocations against roughly three or four actual, with the mutation driving it to twenty-six. The margin is wide, but it is the one assertion in this file sensitive to standard-library allocation behaviour rather than to our own code.","body":{"commit":"53bfe4d5f81043799398abef31d4760612bf5b18","path":"internal/kernel"}}